      "source": [
        "# Python basics\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "This chapter introduces the fundamental concepts and basic syntax of the Python programming language. All the code covered here is part of the standard Python language and does not require any additional packages or libraries. Thus the Python discussed here forms the foundation for not only analyzing data in Python, but also for writing any kind of Python code. \n",
        "\n",
        "While the chapter covers several different key concepts and syntax of Python, we focus on a subset of features that are most central for data analysis, rather than covering the full range of Python's capabilities.  Becoming proficient in the basic Python covered in this chapter will be important as a basis for writing code in subsequent chapters, so make sure to practice and understand these concepts thoroughly.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "Two foundational Python tools — **loops** (for repeating an action many times) and **writing your own functions** — are intentionally *not* covered here. Because the array and table tools introduced in the next several chapters often let us operate on a whole dataset at once, we rarely need loops at first, so we postpone these topics until @sec-iteration-functions, once you've seen why they are useful. You can read the book straight through without them until that point.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "By the end of this chapter, you should be comfortable with writing basic Python code, performing simple calculations, and understanding how Python represents and manipulates different types of data. These foundational skills will prepare you for more advanced topics in data analysis that are covered in the rest of the book. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Expressions \n",
        "\n",
        "A **Python expression** is **any piece of code that produces a value.**. For example, the following is an expression that simply creates the number 21."
      ],
      "id": "0d001b44"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "21"
      ],
      "id": "39270c79",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Similarly, an expression could be a series of mathematical operations that evaluate to a number. For example, if we want to add 5 plus 2 and then multiply the result by 6 we can write:"
      ],
      "id": "801a8989"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "6 * (5 + 2) "
      ],
      "id": "dd763415",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As mentioned above, the defining features of a *python expression* is that it produces a value. Expressions are one of the fundamental building blocks of data analysis and they will appear frequently throughout this book. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-expressions}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "What would happen if we remove the parenthesis from the expression we ran above and instead run `6 * 5 + 2`. See if you can predict what the result will be and then try it out in Python by running the code in a code cell and see if you get the result you predicted. \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-expressions collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "ec4490a6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "6 * 5 + 2"
      ],
      "id": "6a9b4e3d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The result is 32, which makes sense because in the standard order of mathematical operations, multiplication occurs before addition so we multiply 6 * 5 and get 30, and then we add 2 to get 32.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mathematical expressions\n",
        "\n",
        "The expressions shown above were all \"mathematical expressions\" because they involve calculating numeric quantities. We can also write statements that will do operations on text and other types of data which we will describe more below. But first, let's explore mathematical expressions a bit more. Below is a table of some of the mathematical operations that are part of Python:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operation        | Symbol | Example     | Result |\n",
        "|------------------|--------|-------------|--------|\n",
        "| Addition         |   +    | 5 + 3       |   8    |\n",
        "| Subtraction      |   -    | 10 - 4      |   6    |\n",
        "| Multiplication   |   *    | 7 * 2       |  14    |\n",
        "| Division         |   /    | 12 / 5      |   2.4  |\n",
        "| Exponentiation   |   **   | 3 ** 2      |   9    |\n",
        "| Remainder        |   %    | 10 % 3      |   1    |\n",
        "\n",
        ": Python mathematical operators {#tbl-math-ops}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python-math_expression}\n",
        "\n",
        "What is the remainder from dividing 365 by 7? Please write some Python code that produces the answer.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python-math_expression collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "b599e3bc"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "365 % 7"
      ],
      "id": "69057c8a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Syntax \n",
        "\n",
        "**Syntax** is the set of rules that defines how Python code **must** be written. One can think of syntax as the grammar of the Python programming language. In order for Python to be able to run your code, it **must** use the correct syntax. If incorrect syntax is used, then one will get a \"syntax error\", and the code will not run.\n",
        "\n",
        "To illustrate this, let's calculate the value of 8 squared ($8^2$) which hopefully you remember is equal to the value of 64. As shown @tbl-math-ops, if we want to take a value `x` to the power `y` (i.e., to calculate $x^y$) we use the syntax `x**y`. So, if we wanted to calculate $8^2$ we would write the following Python code: "
      ],
      "id": "99d745f8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "8**2"
      ],
      "id": "ad00d20a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Since we have written the correct syntax, the code runs and the result of 64 is calculated as expected. \n",
        "\n",
        "However, if we accidentially put an extra space between the two `*` symbols, Python will not know how to interpret the expression and we will get a syntax error as shown below:\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"html\"}"
      ],
      "id": "a533acb5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| error: true\n",
        "\n",
        "8* *2"
      ],
      "id": "4a64f8cf",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "<!-- Note: The code above should always be evalauted to show the output error message (i.e., I should not use `#| eval: false`). However, there is a bug in quarto rendering the ^ from a Python error message, so if I evaluate this cell the book will not render to a pdf, although it will render to a website.  -->\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"pdf\"}"
      ],
      "id": "ec839ca6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| error: true\n",
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "\n",
        "8* *2"
      ],
      "id": "10fee928",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "<!-- If the failure to show the error message presists, I should insert an image that shows the error message. Alternatively, seems like if I check out a new template repo and add the files it might fix whatever is causing the problem.  -->\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "When there is a syntax error, Python will print out `SyntaxError` and give you an indication where the syntax error has occurred using a `^` symbol.^[The reason this is a syntax error is because Python interprets a single `*` symbol as a multiplication symbol. Thus it is trying to multiply 8 by another multiplication symbol `*`, which gives an error since one can only multiply two numbers together.] As we can see here, Python is trying to show that the syntax error has occurred due to the extra space between the `*` symbols.\n",
        "\n",
        "The ability to be able to spot and fix syntax errors is a fundamental skill you will develop as become proficient in analyzing data in Python. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Assignment statements\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "An *assignment statement* is a line of code that is used to store a value in a named **variable**. We can then refer back to this variable name to retrieve the value we have stored. \n",
        "\n",
        "To assign a value to a variable we use the `=` symbol. For example, the following code assigns the value `10` to the variable `a`:"
      ],
      "id": "89f6d862"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "a = 10"
      ],
      "id": "fbb88af8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can then refer back to the variable `a` later in our code to retrive the stored value. For example, if we just write `a` by itself on the last line of our Python code cell, it will print out the value stored in `a`. "
      ],
      "id": "f3b715e1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "a"
      ],
      "id": "aad6d263",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As we can see, the value printed out is `10` which is the value we had previously stored in the name `a`. \n",
        "\n",
        "If we were to assign the name `a` to another value, it will overwrite the previously stored value and `a` will store the new value."
      ],
      "id": "823ce04c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "a = 21\n",
        "a"
      ],
      "id": "c00bdd14",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can also do mathematical operations on values stored in variables, such as adding and multiplying variables together. For example, we can assign the variable `h` to store the value 24, and the variable `d` to store the value 7, and then we can multiply these together and store the result in the variable `t`."
      ],
      "id": "bdd94f9c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "h = 24\n",
        "d = 7\n",
        "t = h * d\n",
        "t"
      ],
      "id": "791c0c91",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-assignment_statements}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "In the above code we calculated `t = h * d`. Which of the following do you think will happen to the value stored in `t` if we change the value of h to 3? I.e., if we run the following code, what do you think it will print out? "
      ],
      "id": "9f6eca1f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "h = 3\n",
        "t"
      ],
      "id": "0c5fa458",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "a. The value of `t` will be change to be 21  (i.e., `7 * 3`).\n",
        "b. The value of `t` will not change and will still contain `168`.\n",
        "c. Something else will happen (e.g., Python will give an error). \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution--python_basics-assignment_statements collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "19c60440"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "h = 3\n",
        "t"
      ],
      "id": "2e576862",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As you can see, the value of `t` did not change. This illustrates an important point that once a value is calculated and stored in a variable it will not change if the variable that were used as part of the calculation are updated! \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Variable names\n",
        "\n",
        "Variable names in Python must follow certain rules:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Must start with a letter (a-z, A-Z) or an underscore (_), but not a number.\n",
        "- Can contain letters, numbers, and underscores.\n",
        "- Cannot contain spaces or special characters (like `@`, `#`, `$`, etc.).\n",
        "- Cannot be a reserved Python keyword that are part of the Python language (like `for`, `if`, `class`, etc.).\n",
        "\n",
        "If these rules are not followed, Python will produce a syntax error\n",
        "\n",
        "It's also important to use meaningful variable names. For example, `t` is technically a valid variable name but it is not descriptive, while `total_hours` is much clearer. Using meaningful names makes your code easier to read and understand.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-min_wage}\n",
        "\n",
        "The minimum wage in the United States in 2025 is $7.25. If someone works 40 hours per week for all 52 weeks in a year, what would there yearly earnings be if they are being paid the minimum wage? Please calculate this quantity by creating *meaningful* (i.e., easy to understand) object names for:\n",
        "  \n",
        "1. The minimum wage amount   \n",
        "2. The number of hours worked in a week\n",
        "3. The number of weeks in a year   \n",
        " \n",
        "Then calculate the total yearly wage and store this result in another meaningful object name, and print out the value stored in this last object. \n",
        "\n",
        "Hint: Using underscores `_` in your object names is highly encouraged to make them more meaningful/readable. \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-min_wage collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "e91dcc77"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "min_wage = 7.25\n",
        "hours_worked_in_a_week = 40\n",
        "weeks_in_a_year = 52 \n",
        "yearly_min_wage_earnings = min_wage * hours_worked_in_a_week * weeks_in_a_year\n",
        "yearly_min_wage_earnings"
      ],
      "id": "43e8a540",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Comments\n",
        "\n",
        "Another very useful feature in Python is the ability to add **comments** to your code. Comments are lines in your code that are ignored by Python when your code runs. They are used to explain what your code is doing, make notes to yourself, or leave instructions for others who may read your code in the future.\n",
        "\n",
        "In Python, you create a comment by starting the line with the `#` symbol. Anything after the `#` on that line will be treated as a comment and not executed.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:"
      ],
      "id": "934e9090"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# The code below calculates the number of seconds in a day\n",
        "seconds_in_a_day = 60 * 60 * 24\n",
        "\n",
        "seconds_in_a_day"
      ],
      "id": "28742439",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We will use comments extensively throughout this book to explain what code is doing and to make our code easier to understand. Adding clear comments is a good habit that will help both you and others who read your code in the future, so we strongly encourage you to add comments liberally for all code you write. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Functions (call expressions)\n",
        "\n",
        "A **function** is a reusable piece of code that performs a specific task. You can think of a function as a \"machine\" that takes some input, does something with it, and then gives you an output. \n",
        "\n",
        "Python comes with many built-in functions that you can use right away, and you can also load in additional functions in packages that other people have written. You can also write own functions, which is a topic we will discuss later in this book. \n",
        "\n",
        "To use a function, you \"call\" it by writing its name followed by parentheses. If the function needs information to do its job, you put that information (called \"arguments\") inside the parentheses.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, the `abs()` function take in a number and returns the absolute value of the number. "
      ],
      "id": "fc1e0ad0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "abs(-10)"
      ],
      "id": "1d31c46d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Some functions can take in multiple arguments. When multiple arguments are provided, they are separated by commas within the parentheses. For example, the `min()` function can take several numbers and will return the smallest one:"
      ],
      "id": "ddbbb628"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "min(10, 2, 87, 5, 90)"
      ],
      "id": "7f676f59",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Another useful function is the `print()` function for displaying multiple pieces of information in a single Jupyter notebook code cell. By default, Jupyter will only display the result of the last line in a code cell. If you want to display multiple values or add custom messages, you can use the `print()` function. \n",
        "\n",
        "For example, the code below will print the number `2` because `print(2)` is called. The number `3` will also be displayed as output because it's the last expression in the cell. If `print(2)` was not used, only `3` would be displayed. The `print()` function is useful when you want to display multiple values from within a single cell or when you want to output values that are not on the last line."
      ],
      "id": "0f0fdd5a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# We need to call print() explicitly here to print the value of \n",
        "# 2 since it is not on the last line of the code cell\n",
        "\n",
        "print(2)  \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# The value of 3 will be printed here without needing to call \n",
        "# the print() function because it is the last line in the cell\n",
        "\n",
        "3\n"
      ],
      "id": "330bd5ed",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-print}\n",
        "\n",
        "Try using the `print()` function to display both a message and a value in the same output. For example, print the message \"The answer is:\" followed by the result of `6 * 7`. \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-print collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "f7b088f8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(\"The answer is:\")\n",
        "6 * 7"
      ],
      "id": "22535919",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# We can also print multiple pieces of text on a single line by \n",
        "# passing multiple arguments to the print() function: \n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"The answer is:\", 6 * 7)"
      ],
      "id": "a4417288",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data types\n",
        "\n",
        "Python is able to process many different types of data, referred to as \"data types\". So far, we have only explored numeric data. Let's continue exploring numerical data in a little more detail and then we will go on to examine other types of data.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Numbers\n",
        "\n",
        "Python uses two different formats to store numerical data known as \"integers\" and \"floating-point numbers\".\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Integers** (`int`): Whole numbers without a decimal point, such as `5`, `-3`, or `1000`.\n",
        "- **Floating-point numbers** (`float`): Numbers that have a decimal point, such as `3.14`, `-0.5`, or `2.0`.\n",
        "\n",
        "We can tell if a number is a floating point number (i.e., a \"float\") by seeing if there is a decimal point at the end of the number when we print out the number. "
      ],
      "id": "2fae78fd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# This is an integer, which we can tell becaues there is no decimal point\n",
        "5"
      ],
      "id": "52a638c2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Although we are dividing two integers, the result is a floating point number\n",
        "# which we can tell becaues there is a decimal point\n",
        "\n",
        "10/2\n"
      ],
      "id": "c6526329",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can also use the `type()` function to check if a number is an integer or a floating point number. "
      ],
      "id": "4240f0f6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# This is a floating point number\n",
        "\n",
        "type(5.0)"
      ],
      "id": "32af3d74",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "When analyzing the data, usually it does not matter if Python is storing a number as an integer or a floating point number since Python does the math sensibly and converts between integers and floating point numbers as needed. However, internally Python is representing these numbers in quite different ways.\n",
        "\n",
        "More importantly, one should be aware that there are some limitations to the way Python stores both integers and floats. In particular, both of these types of numbers are represented using a finite amount of memory, so there is a largest number integer that can be represented and a limit to the precision of floating-point numbers. For most practical purposes, these limits are very large, but you may encounter issues with extremely large numbers or with floating-point arithmetic where results are not exactly as expected due to rounding errors.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, if we multiply integers that are very large, we can get a `ValueError` which indicates that Python is running into problems representing the result as an integer.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"html\"}"
      ],
      "id": "62427e0b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| error: true\n",
        "\n",
        "# There is a limited size to integers (although the size is pretty large)\n",
        "\n",
        "1234567 ** 890 \n"
      ],
      "id": "d6ff9cfd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"pdf\"}"
      ],
      "id": "d1c315d9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| error: true\n",
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "\n",
        "# There is a limited size to integers (although the size is pretty large)\n",
        "\n",
        "1234567 ** 890 \n"
      ],
      "id": "2e1c99e1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Note: The code above should always be evalauted to show the output error message (i.e., I should not use `eval: format: false`). However, there is a bug in quarto rendering this to pdf on my Windows computer, so if I evaluate this cell the book will not render to a pdf, although it will render to a website and it will render to a pdf on my mac.  -->\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Similarly, if we try to create a floating-point number with too many decimal points the number will be truncated, although no error is given, so one needs to be careful if very high precision is needed in a calculation."
      ],
      "id": "818c1c03"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# There is a limited precision to floating point numbers so the last digits are truncated\n",
        "\n",
        ".12345678901234567890123456789 \n"
      ],
      "id": "241fd501",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can also convert numbers between integers and floating point numbers using the `int()` and `float()` functions. When converting from a floating point number to an integer using the `int()` function, one needs to be aware that the decimal part of the number will be removed (i.e., rounded down to the closest integer)\n"
      ],
      "id": "9b830b7e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Convert an integer to a floating point number.  We can see the conversion worked because the number is printed with a decimal point. \n",
        "\n",
        "float(5)"
      ],
      "id": "7704d7df",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Convert a floating point number to an integer. Note that the decimal part of the number is removed\n",
        "\n",
        "int(3.14159)"
      ],
      "id": "e382e858",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Finally, one should be aware that Python sometimes prints out numbers using scientific notation. Scientific notation is a way of writing very large or very small numbers more compactly, using the letter `e` to indicate \"times ten to the power of.\" For example, `2.5e6` means \\(2.5 \\times 10^{6}\\), or `2,500,000`.  Similarly,  `3e-09` means \\(3 \\times 10^{-9}\\), or `0.000000003`. Python will automatically use this notation when displaying numbers that are extremely large or small.\n"
      ],
      "id": "366221fd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# The output is in scientific notation\n",
        "30 / 4000000000 "
      ],
      "id": "b23026b6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-ints_and_floats}\n",
        "\n",
        "Take the square root of 12 and then square the result; i.e., calculate $(\\sqrt{12})^2$. Does Python return the correct result? \n",
        "\n",
        "Hint: Note that you can calculate the square root of a number by taking a number to the 1/2 power; i.e., $\\sqrt{12} = 12^{0.5}$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-ints_and_floats collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "e556e114"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "(12**.5)**2"
      ],
      "id": "e7926b96",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As you can see, there is slight imprecision here so we get a result of 11.999999999999998 rather than a value of 12. \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Character strings\n",
        "\n",
        "A **character string** (or simply \"string\") is a sequence of characters that are used to represent text, such as words, sentences, or any other sequence of characters. Strings in Python are enclosed in either single quotes (`'...'`) or double quotes (`\"...\"`). However, your string must start and end with the same quote type; i.e., if the string starts with a single quote it also ends with a single quote, and the same for double quotes.\n",
        "\n",
        "The following are valid strings in Python:"
      ],
      "id": "514f0441"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "'This is a valid Python string with single quotes'\n",
        "\n",
        "\"This is another valid Python string using double quotes.\""
      ],
      "id": "b93547c7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "While using single or double quotes gives the same result, there are cases where it is natural to use one over the other. For example, if your string contains an apostrophe (single quote), it's easier to use double quotes:"
      ],
      "id": "99446cdd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "\"This string contains an apostrophe: it's easy to read.\""
      ],
      "id": "3aa741e8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "And if your string contains double quotes, it is easier to single quotes when creating your string:"
      ],
      "id": "848ef3f7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "'She said, \"Hello, world!\"'"
      ],
      "id": "7e6f8e2c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can also perform operations on strings, such as concatenation (joining strings together). For example, to join two strings together, you can use the `+` operator:"
      ],
      "id": "c10cac37"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "\"water\" + \"mellon\""
      ],
      "id": "174c89cd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Note that the `+` operator we have used to concatenate strings is the same `+` operator we used to add numbers. This illustrates an important principle that an operator can behave differently depending on the type of data it is used with. In Python, this is called \"operator overloading.\" For numbers, `+` performs addition, while for strings, it performs concatenation (joining the strings together).\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-strings}\n",
        "\n",
        "Above we have seen that the `+` operator can behave differently depending on whether it is operating on numbers or strings. We have also seen that `*` operator is used to multiple two numbers together. Do you think that the `*` operator will also work on strings? Please write down, or say outloud whether you think the  `*` operator will work on strings, then see if your prediction is correct by running the following code: "
      ],
      "id": "ca6e0d40"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "\n",
        "'ha' * 5"
      ],
      "id": "a1f99eaf",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-strings collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "df728c96"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "'ha' * 5"
      ],
      "id": "992dd6d4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As you can see, the `*` operator works on strings by repeating the string the specified number of times. In this case, `'ha' * 5` produces `'hahahahaha'` (which is very amusing). \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "####  String conversions\n",
        "\n",
        "We can also convert strings into numbers and numbers into strings. To convert strings into numbers we can again use the `int()` and `float()` functions, but this time we are passing a string as the argument to these functions."
      ],
      "id": "8986212a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "int(\"42\")      # Converts the string \"42\" to the integer 42"
      ],
      "id": "17a85220",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "float(\"3.14\")  # Converts the string \"3.14\" to the float 3.14"
      ],
      "id": "b24f49b0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can see that the output from running these functions are numbers since the output is not in quotes.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "We can convert a number into a string using the `str()` function. "
      ],
      "id": "7d5bad5c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "str(2.5)       # Converts the float 2.5 to the string \"2.5\""
      ],
      "id": "f8274e28",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can see that the output from running this function is a string since the output is in quotes.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-string_conversion}\n",
        "\n",
        "Do the following two lines of code produce the same result? \n",
        "\n",
        "- `10 + 20`\n",
        "- `int(\"10\" + \"20\")`\n",
        "\n",
        "Explain your reasoning then try it in Python to verify your answer is correct.  \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-string_conversion collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "23a4bf96"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(10 + 20)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(int(\"10\" + \"20\"))"
      ],
      "id": "ab4eb01a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As we can see, the result of running these two pieces of code are different. The first line of code produce the value of 30 since we are simply adding the integers `10` and `20` together. \n",
        "\n",
        "The second line of code first concatenates the strings `\"10\"` and `\"20\"` together to create the string `\"1020\"` and then converts it to the integer `1020`, which is clearly different from the integer `30`.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "#### f-strings\n",
        "\n",
        "An **f-string** (short for \"formatted string literal\") is a way to embed the values of variables or expressions inside a string. To create an f-string, put the letter `f` before the opening quote, and then include curly braces `{}` around the variables or expressions you want to insert.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:"
      ],
      "id": "a6de3013"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "name = \"Methuselah\"\n",
        "age = 969\n",
        "\n",
        "f\"My name is {name} and I am {age} years old.\""
      ],
      "id": "485d7dce",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-fstrings}\n",
        "\n",
        "Create three variables: `name`, `age`, and `favorite_color`, and assign them your own name, age and favorite color.\n",
        "Then, use an f-string to print a sentence like:  \n",
        "`\"My name is <name>, I am <age> years old, and my favorite color is <favorite_color>.\"`\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-fstrings collapse=\"true\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "My solution (at the time of writing this book) is below."
      ],
      "id": "ca1a218e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "name = \"Ethan\"\n",
        "age = 45\n",
        "favorite_color = \"red\"\n",
        "\n",
        "f\"My name is {name}, I am {age} years old, and my favorite color is {favorite_color}.\""
      ],
      "id": "d5c9ee67",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "#### String methods\n",
        "\n",
        "A **method** is a function that is attached to a piece of data ^[Or to be more precise, a method is a function attached to an object]. There are a number of \"string methods\" which allow you to perform specific operations on strings, such as changing their case, finding substrings, or replacing text.\n",
        "\n",
        "You call a method by writing the string (or variable containing a string), followed by a dot (`.`), the method name, and parentheses. For example, the `.upper()` method returns a copy of the string with all letters converted to uppercase:"
      ],
      "id": "53a0c4d5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "\"hello\".upper()"
      ],
      "id": "4081d5cb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here is a table of some particularly useful string methods. Each method returns a new string that is modified as described below. \n",
        "\n",
        "|   Method             | Example                     | Description                 | Result                |\n",
        "|----------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------|\n",
        "|   `.upper()`         | `\"hello\".upper()`         | Converts all characters to uppercase        | `'HELLO'`    |\n",
        "|   `.lower()`         | `\"HELLO\".lower()`         | Converts all characters to lowercase        | `'hello'`    |\n",
        "|   `.strip()`         | `\"  hello  \".strip()`     | Removes leading and trailing whitespace     | `'hello'`    |\n",
        "|  `.replace(a, b)`    | `\"ha\".replace(\"a\", \"o\")`  | Replace all occurrences of `a` with `b`    | `'ho'`        |\n",
        "|   `.count(x)`        | `\"banana\".count(\"a\")`     | Counts the number of occurrences of `x`     | `3`          |\n",
        "|   `.zfill(n)`        | `\"42\".zfill(5)`           | Pads the string with zeros to reach length `n` | `'00042'` |\n",
        "| `.find(x)`       | `\"hello\".find(\"e\")`       | Returns the index of the first occurrence of `x` | `1`         |\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Could potentially move this section to a \"data cleaning\" part of the book to keep things simple when students are just starint out --> \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-string_methods1}\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we have the string `my_sentence = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"`. Please use string methods to do the following:\n",
        "1. Count how many times the letter `e` appears in this sentence. \n",
        "2. Find the index of the first occurance of the letter `z`.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-string_methods1 collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "1878a4db"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "my_sentence = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"\n",
        "\n",
        "print(my_sentence.count(\"e\"))\n",
        "\n",
        "my_sentence.find(\"z\")"
      ],
      "id": "dd06415e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-string_methods2}\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose again we have the string `my_sentence = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"`. Please use the `.replace()` method to replace the word \"dog\" with the word \"canine\". Does the string in the `my_sentence` variable change? If not, how could you update the string in the `my_sentence` variable to so that it contains the string \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy canine\"?  \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-string_methods2 collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "124e3e91"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "my_sentence = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"\n",
        "\n",
        "print(my_sentence.replace(\"dog\", \"canine\"))\n",
        "\n",
        "# notice that the variable my_sentence still has the original string\n",
        "print(my_sentence)\n",
        "\n",
        "# to update the string in the variable my_sentence we can do the following\n",
        "my_sentence = my_sentence.replace(\"dog\", \"canine\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(my_sentence)"
      ],
      "id": "e72496df",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Booleans\n",
        "\n",
        "A **Boolean** is a data type that can have only two possible values: `True` or `False`. Booleans are used to represent truth values and are very useful for making decisions in your code.\n",
        "\n",
        "You can create Boolean values directly by writing `True` or `False` (note the capital letters):"
      ],
      "id": "dc05b89a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Create the Boolean True\n",
        "True"
      ],
      "id": "81950a5c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The Boolean `True` is also the same as the integer 1, the Boolean `False` is the same as the integer 0. This means we can do arithmetic on Booleans such as:"
      ],
      "id": "70da2009"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "True + False + True"
      ],
      "id": "6faff920",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We will use the fact that Booleans can be treated the integers 1 and 0 later in some of our analyses. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Comparisons\n",
        "\n",
        "Comparison operators are used to compare values and produce Boolean results. For example, we can assess whether one number is greater than another number:"
      ],
      "id": "ddba9fd4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "5 > 3"
      ],
      "id": "1247c8ee",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "If we want to compare whether two values are the same, we use two equal signs `==`. For example, we can see that indeed strings that are created using single quotes are the same as strings created using double quotes by running the following code. "
      ],
      "id": "d1f9b518"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "\"Octothorpe\" == 'Octothorpe'"
      ],
      "id": "c4d9c1f6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here are some common comparison operators:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operator | Description                  | Example      | Result   |\n",
        "|----------|------------------------------|--------------|----------|\n",
        "| ==       | Equal to                     | 5 == 5       | True     |\n",
        "| !=       | Not equal to                 | 5 != 3       | True     |\n",
        "| >        | Greater than                 | 7 > 2        | True     |\n",
        "| <        | Less than                    | 3 < 1        | False    |\n",
        "| >=       | Greater than or equal to     | 4 >= 4       | True     |\n",
        "| <=       | Less than or equal to        | 2 <= 5       | True     |\n",
        "\n",
        ": Python comparison operators\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-string_vs_int}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Is the string `\"99\"` equal to the integer `99` in Python? Also is `1` equal to `True`? Use the equal to operator (`==`) to do these comparisons and see what result you get.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-string_vs_int collapse=\"true\"}"
      ],
      "id": "4850d0ec"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(\"99\" == 99)\n",
        "\n",
        "1 == True\n"
      ],
      "id": "0a3ad506",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As we can see, the string `\"99\"` is not equal to the integer `99` which once again showing strings and integers are not the same thing. \n",
        "\n",
        "Conversely, the integer `1` is equal to the Boolean `True` again showing that these are identical. \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data structures \n",
        "\n",
        "Python provides several built-in data structures that allow you to store and organize collections of data. The most common ones are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Lists**: Ordered, mutable collections of items.\n",
        "- **Tuples**: Ordered, immutable collections of items.\n",
        "- **Dictionaries**: Unordered collections of key-value pairs.\n",
        "\n",
        "We will introduce each of these data structures in the following sections.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Lists\n",
        "\n",
        "A **list** is an ordered collection of items that can be changed. Lists can contain any type of data, including numbers, strings, or even other lists. Lists are created by placing items inside square brackets `[]`, separated by commas.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example:"
      ],
      "id": "fbd60f9f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "my_list = [1, 2, 3, \"a\", \"b\", \"c\", True]\n",
        "\n",
        "my_list"
      ],
      "id": "a2783d70",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can access individual items in a list by their position (called the \"index\") using square brackets. In Python, indexing starts at 0, so the first item is at index 0, the second at index 1, and so on.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, to get the first item in `my_list`, we would use:"
      ],
      "id": "112958fa"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "my_list[0]"
      ],
      "id": "2dc802c8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This returns `1` since the first element in the list (i.e., the element at position 0) is the integer 1. \n",
        "\n",
        "Likewise, we can get the 6th element (remembering that indexing starts at 0) using: "
      ],
      "id": "e4533169"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "my_list[5]"
      ],
      "id": "5de75360",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can also change the value of an item in a list by assigning a new value to a specific index. For example, `my_list[0] = 100` will change the first item in the list to 100."
      ],
      "id": "b39c01ea"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "my_list[0] = 100\n",
        "\n",
        "my_list"
      ],
      "id": "c2c4de1d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The fact that we can change items of a list is what makes lists \"mutable.\" This means you can update, add, or remove elements after the list has been created. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**List Methods**\n",
        "\n",
        "Lists also have a number of useful methods which we will use throughout this book including: \n",
        "\n",
        "| Method           | Example                   | Description                                      | Result / Effect                  |\n",
        "|------------------|--------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n",
        "| `.append(x)`     | `my_list.append(7)`      | Adds item `x` to the end of the list             | `[... , 7]`                      |\n",
        "| `.index(x)`      | `my_list.index('b')`     | Returns index of first occurrence of `x`         | Index of `'b'`                   |\n",
        "| `.count(x)`      | `my_list.count(3)`       | Counts occurrences of item `x`                   | Number of times `3` appears      |\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, we can use the `append()` method to adds new items to the end of a list (which again illustrates that lists are mutable):\n"
      ],
      "id": "9a454fcb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "my_list.append(\"zzz\")\n",
        "\n",
        "my_list"
      ],
      "id": "42d580db",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-tip title=\"Exercise\" #exercise-python_basics-lists}\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we have a list of numbers: `my_numbers = [5, 10, 15, 5, 20]`. Please use Python do the following:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. From just looking at the list, write down what you think the index of the number `15` is in the list. Then\n",
        "   check your answer by running the code to find the index of `15`.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Use Python to count how many times the number `5` appears in the list.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Add the number `25` to the end of the list.  \n",
        "\n",
        "4. Change the first element of the list to `1`.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. Print the updated list to see the changes.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note title=\"Solution\" #solution-python_basics-lists collapse=\"true\"}"
